No, I didn't. I've never made such a claim. I've merely noted that
even if your cholesterol levels remain stable irrespective of diet
you may want to do something about your fat intake.

However, I completely acknowledge that some people are resistant to
even the worst lifestyles.

In fact, there appear to be some individuals who's circulatory
system has made astonishing adaptations to a high fat high cholesterol
diet even with nicotine stress added -- people with the so-called "Churchill
gene", named for Winston Churchill. I've got a friend who was at Rockefeller
U. when they were doing research on that very topic. They found, however,
that even in such individuals a high fat meal can reduce the capacity of
the blood to carry oxygen by 20-30% for a period after the meal and could
not come to any conclusions about various kinds of cancer risks.

Yeah, you are completely right -- there are individuals out there who can
eat nothing but pork fatback day and night, chain smoke and swill whiskey,
and they'll live to a ripe old age doing it. However, at the moment, there
is only one way to know for sure that you are one such individual, and
thats to try doing it. Finding out the hard way that you AREN'T such a
person is unlikely to be pleasant.

Since you can't yet reliably determine that you are one of those individuals
who can do anything that they like diet wise, you are playing russian
roulette with your health. I'd say its a smarter idea to eat healthy
food instead. There is a chance that you are needlessly giving up 
your steaks, but given the lack of ability to determine this for sure,
are you sure you are willing to take the risk?

