San Jose State University Professor Gordon Edwards (Biology Department),
widely regarded as one of the foremost authorities on DDT in the world, has
frequently eaten large quantities of DDT at press conferences.  A prisoner
study, involving ingestion of one pure tablespoon of DDT per day for almost
a year, showed no adverse effects.  It was used sucessfully by the Mayo
Clinic for cancer chemotherapy until it was banned.
 
DDT was given a clean bill of health by the EPA when it was subjected to
scientific testing.  William Ruckelshaus, EPA agency chief at the time,
openly stated that DDT must be banned for political reasons; that this would
give the EPA the influence to attack other chemicals.   
 
DDT is currently being used by most of the world and in much higher doses
than was ever the case here.  It has been estimated that hundreds of
millions of people would have starved to death if it had been universally
banned.
 
Purported environmental problems have not held up to analysis.  For example,
DDT has been blamed for thinning eggshells.  Subsequent investigation proved
that the actual cause was Newcastle's disease.  Tests done by
"environmentalists" on soil hermetically sealed for centuries by a British
scientific society showed traces of DDT; their faulty methodology allowed
false positive readings.
 
No satisfactory alternatives are as environmentally safe.  Gypsy moth
infestations are killing old-growth forests, but irrational policy prevents
killing them with DDT, the only safe, yet efficacious method.
 
