From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL: Fraud

Timothy C. May asks:
>should anarchocapitalists support laws against "fraud"? ...
>Written, enforceable _contracts_ are another matter altogether ...
>For example, a written contract to find water at a site is easily
>enforeable: if water is found, payment is made...else, no payment.
>(This is easily handled through third-party escrow services). The
>issue of whether a water dowser is a fraud is never a legal issue.

I seem to be agreeing with Tim a lot lately.  I also want to unbundle
by methods for avoiding being lied to from my more widely shared legal
institutions.  Contracts to be adjudicated by widely shared law should
focus on fairly literal promises, and avoid making judgements about
subtler points like whether water dowsing works.  I do think there is
room for courts infering that implicitly some contract assumed that
some party was who they appeared to be, but such inferences could be
overridden by explicit contractual language to the contrary.  Fraud,
like libel and rudeness, should not be dealth with at the same level
as raw force.

Robin Hanson

