Date: Thu, 28 Jan 93 12:20:10 EST
From: lubkin@apollo.hp.com
Subject: Nutrients

I poked around my nutritional books last night, and got firmer
data on B-12 and zinc.

B-12.  Perry is right on the B-12 good news.  You don't need 
much -- 3 mcg a day is both the RDA and many nutritionists' 
recommendations, any reasonable whole-grain vegan diet will
have more than that, and it is the only water-soluble 
vitamin that is stored in your body.

There are two points of bad news.  First, stress increases
your body's use of B-12, but no one says by how much.  But
the real gotcha is that it is very hard for the body to
absorb B-12.  When it is used therapeutically, it is often
administered with gastic juices, which seem to aid 
absorption.  

It may be that Perry's way of taking B-12 has a superior
absorption rate.  I don't know.  I'd be interested in
anything you (Perry) know about it.

What >I< do is take a sublingual formulation of B-12
around 300 mcg a day.  That is a lot, but it's one small,
cheap pill, I'm fairly assured of getting enough, and all 
the research I've seen indicates that B-12 is incredibly
non-toxic, even in doses 10,000,000 times a therapeutic
dose.

Zinc.  As I said, sunflower seeds are really good.  The
best vegan source is sesame seeds (sprinkle them in your
cooking), but it's in almost everything.  Best is seeds,
followed by legumes and nuts, and then grain.

BTW, I've never seen an independent source for it, but
my father (who, like me, rarely remembers where he read
something) claims that 50% of the dry weight of the 
testes is zinc.  That seems high to me, but I can 
confirm that a zinc deficiency can lead to testicular
ailments.

Folic acid.  Nobody talks about it much, but I was
reminded of it when I looked up B-12, since B-12 can't
be used without sufficient folic acid.  The nutritional
books are funny.  On one hand, they all say that most
Americans (especially women) have a folic acid deficiency.
On the other hand, they act like all you need is enough
lettuce and you're fine.

I looked up folic acid contents, and it looks like 
it's awful hard to get enough without either having
4 or 5 cups of vegetables (peas, okra, corn, etc)
or 1/2 pound of liver every day.

And that's just talking 400 mcg.  There are indications
that you need a lot more.  Roger Williams, who discovered
folic acid, recommends 2000 mcg.  And there is no known
toxic dose.

Meanwhile, the Feds limited folic acid supplements
to 400 mcg because if you took too much folic acid, it
could mask the symptoms of pernicious anemia (B-12 
deficiency).  That is not now a problem, due to improved
diagnostic options.

So take some folic acid.  Bronson sells 400 mcg for 
about a penny each.

Now, a couple comments.  As the discussion on cholesterol
and diet showed us, people are very different in terms
of the health effects of their diet.  They're just as
different in terms of their individual need for nutrients
and in their body's ability to absorb those nutrients.

Don't just read books.  Find a competent clinical 
nutritionist, who will take a comprehensive history and 
arrange the lab work to ascertain your personal needs.  
He'll do things like take cell samples and analyze them 
to see how much of what you take actually gets where 
it's needed.

My guy, who is a naturopath, a homeopath, and a clinical
nutritionist, found, for example, that I have pyroluria,
