Timothy May asked the rhetorical question:

    ". . . should anarchocapitalists support laws against
    'fraud'"?

Though Tim answers in the negative, I think his examples and
analysis miss the point.  I think there are aspects to this
question which must addressed before any of the examples become
relevant.

The first is definitional.  Until we have a mutually acceptable
definition for "fraud" we are just chasing our own tails.
Unfortunately, informal definitions are too varied and too
imprecise to give us what we need for a discussion.  Under them,
everything mentioned in previous posts could arguably be called
"fraud."

If, however, we define by essentials (thank you Ayn) we can
distinguish fraud from "falsity," "untruth" and so on.  In law,
several "elements" must be present before an act is considered
fraudulent.  (This is so-called *black letter law*, and I'm doing
it from memory.  Please don't hold my feet to the fire if I miss
something.)  A person commits fraud if he KNOWINGLY (1) makes
MATERIAL MISREPRESENTATION (2) upon which another person RELIES
(3) to his DETRIMENT (4).  If any of the four elements are
absent, there is no fraud.  (There might be other issues, but not
fraud.)  Using this definition, lets examine some of the examples
that have been offered in various posts.

    "A few months back, around the time of the U.S.
    election, someone here was commenting that candidates
    who call themselves "libertarians," but who are not (in
    some sense, or members of the LP), should be _sued for
    fraud_. I was very uncomfortable with this notion."

Same here, Tim.  I don't know if *any* of the elements of fraud
are present here.


    "Realize that if fraud laws are actually enforced, then
    essentially all religions . . .  will get shut down.
    Ditto for so many other areas that I cannot begin to
    list them here!"

Cool your jets, Tim.  (Sandy's tip of the day:  Exclamation
points are the written equivalent of pounding on the table.) I
think most religious folks are sincere in their beliefs.  So,
usually no element "1", probably some "2", but who knows? Usually
there is some "3", but the "4" is hard to prove.  But when Jim
Baker oversells his theme park membership by 2-300%, that's
another story.  Fraud on the face of it, wouldn't you say?

The second aspect we must address is that of appropriate remedy.
Merely because we have identify a fraud, does not mean we may
take any action we please to rectify or punish it.

    "The trends in our society toward "protecting" people by
    passing all kinds of laws about truthfulness, fraud,
    etc., are incredibly statist."

True, but irrelevant.  The State is statist--by definition--even
when it sets out to do something "good."  I will not defend the
State's paternalistic policies.

    "I'm not sure how a PPL system would handle this. I
    suppose people could contract with agencies to "shield
    them" from the likes of religious zealots and magicians,
    to screen their phone calls, and to review all
    contacts."

The concept of fraud does not lend itself to prior restraint.
This does not mean, however, than it should go unchallenged in a
PPL or anarchocapitalists setting.  Try and imagine what could or
should happen *after* a fraud has occurred.

    "Written, enforceable _contracts_ are another matter
    altogether . . .  For example, a written contract to
    find water at a site is easily enforceable: if water is
    found, payment is made...else, no payment. (This is
    easily handled through third-party escrow services). The
    issue of whether a water dowser is a fraud is never a
    legal issue."

Could the same be said if the contract were for rain making?  The
draught has devastated Tim May's farm.  Slick Sandy shows up and
signs a contract with Tim promising to use psychic powers to make
it rain for US$100,000.  Tim puts the money in escrow.  Slick
Sandy dances in Tim's field.  If it rains, Sandy gets the money.
No questions asked.  If it doesn't rain, Sandy goes to the next
farm.  Maybe some hint of fraud there? Too obvious, you say.
What about unorthodox medical cures?  With a little thought, we
could all come up with true-to-life examples of how even the most
skeptical could be defrauded.  Please don't think it can't happen
to you.  It IS possible to defraud intelligent, aware people.

Also, for some time, I have been disturbed by the way libertarian
types and others glibly invoke such "solutions" as "written,
enforceable _contracts_" to govern human interactions.  The
underlying assumption is that it is possible to have a total
explicit, unambiguous contract.  Sorry, it can't be done in the
real world.  Contracts--written or oral--ALWAYS involve implicit
assumptions.  Unfortunately, a contract is often little more than
a license to sue.

